sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2019-boundary-08> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In tadpoles, depolarizing a specific cell population is sufficient to cause metastatic-like transformation of normal melanocytes in genetically normal animals, while optogenetic or constitutive hyperpolarization prevents human oncogenes from inducing tumours.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Depolarization induces and hyperpolarization prevents tumour-like changes in tadpoles";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Levin M, “The Computational Boundary of a \"Self\": Developmental Bioelectricity Drives Multicellularity and Scale-Free Cognition”, Frontiers in psychology (2019), doi:10.3389/fpsyg.2019.02688; section “Multicellularity vs. Cancer: The Shifting Boundary of the Self”. Scope: Xenopus laevis tadpoles; melanocytes and human-oncogene-induced tumours"
}
